===Prequel===
The prequel of the story was aired on KTK as a Christmas special on December 25, 1982, with the same characters from the Taisen drama appearing on it. The story revolves on how Shigetomo was raised when he was a kid. The response to the prequel was overwhelmingly positive, with the final hour of the 4-hour special reaching a Christmas record of 50.8%.

====First Hour====
In the first hour, the story begins in Yokosuka, the traditional home of the Honda clan. Honda Shigetada (Takeda Tetsuya), then a young samurai under the Honda clan, was forced to marry a hostage called "Hatsu no kata" (Natsume Masako) as part of a mutual agreement between the Ogasawara and the Anjō clan. Hatsune's father was [[Anjō Motonobu]] (Kitaoji Kin'ya), a leader of a small clan just east of Suemori at the mountains of Ise. The mutual agreement between the two clans was done to ensure that the Honda clan would not be attacked from the northeast by other clans such as the Taktoyo and the Ono. In exchange, the Honda clan will let someone in the Anjō household to marry a high-ranking officer of the Honda clan, in which Hatsu selected Shigetada. At the beginning of the ceremony, Shigetada seemed very frustrated with the terms of his marriage; as time went by he and Hatsu did not get along with each other, and have not yet consummated their marriage. 

Both of them believed that it would not be beneficial for them to continue their marriage in the end once the Anjō clan become assimilated by the powerful Ogasawara clan or destroyed for not abiding with the conditions of the stronger clan nearby such as the Ono and the Taketoyo. With Taketoyo Sadahiro's health quickly deteriorating over the span of two years without producing a proper successor of his clan, it was known to the clan that there would not be a male successor thus sealing the fate of the Taketoyo clan. The Anjō must make a decision to amalgamate its holdings with either the Ono or the Ogasawara since these clans are perceived to be more powerful than the Honda clan. 

Hatsu told Shigetada what had happened with the fate of the Anjō clan and that she could not continue her marriage to him because of the weakness of her clan, which means that she could no longer fulfill the duties her father had originally given for her. Shigetada objected, telling Hatsu that while she may not be able to fulfill her duties as an Anjō, he had been rethinking about her place in the clan and had additionally fallen affectionate with her during her stay at the Honda residence. Shigetada then added to her that she could not go back to the Anjō that easily as she is now part of the Honda clan and therefore must also function as a member of the Honda clan. Hatsu first reluctantly accepted to stay as Shigetada's partner but as she noticed Shigetada's true affection towards her, she accepted to become his primary partner. 

Motonobu, who have heard the news of Honda Shigetada's gratitude, came to Suemori to officially become his vassal. He plays a part of raising Honda Shigetomo during the events of the drama, before tragically passing away at the Battle of Mogami.

====Second Hour====
Shigetada's adopted father Shigetatsu (Takaichi Osamu) questioned Shigetada's decision to keep Hatsune. He reprimanded Shigetada and requested to annull his marriage to Hatsune since it will hinder in their negotiations with the other clans in Yokosuka. Shigetada refused since it was his father's decision in the first place to be married to Hatsune. Shigetatsu was angered by his argument and brandished his sword, but when he saw the look of Shigetada's eyes, a flashback appeared in front of him, where a young Shigetomo was in tears at a temple. He had been abandoned there by Ogasawara Terumasa for refusing to be a participant of his clan; Shigetatsu asked why he was under the Ogasawara in the first place and the young page replied that Terumasa had told him that he was not really his son so he placed him at the temple. After the brief flashback, he found out that his sword is on top of Shigetada's back. The sharp blade of the sword had cut a small portion of his ''yukata'' but Shigetada kept his calm. Shigetatsu went back on his seating position looking at him. He can see Shigetada's tear drops in the ground but he did not make any noise. He affirmed Shigetada that he can keep his marriage with Hatsune. Shigetada kept his composure until only Shigetatsu and him are left on the room.

====Third Hour: "The Shadow of the Antlers"====
The third hour of the special jumps forward to 1582, focusing on the birth and early childhood of Shigetomo (played as a child by [[Ryūnosuke Kamiki]]). While the eldest son, Shigemune (later [[Prince Takahito]]), is celebrated as the "Imperial Light" destined for the Chrysanthemum Throne, Shigetomo is born during a violent thunderstorm. The clan midwife predicts a turbulent life, claiming he was "born with a shadow on his face" ({{nihongo|Kage-no-kao|影の顔}}).

Neglected by his ambitious father Shigetada, the young Shigetomo finds a surrogate father figure in his maternal grandfather, the massive [[Anjō Motonobu]] (played by [[Kitaoji Kin'ya]]). Scenes depict Motonobu carrying the boy on his shoulders near the rivers of Mikata, teaching him that true strength lies not in rigidity, but in adaptability.
{{Quote|text="Listen, Naiki. The rock breaks the water, but the water wears down the rock. Do not be the rock. Be the river."|sign=Anjō Motonobu to the young Shigetomo}}

To secure a fragile alliance, the young Shigetomo (childhood name "Naiki") is eventually sent as a hostage to the Ogasawara clan. It is here he meets fellow hostage [[Ii Naotaka]] ("Manchiyo"). The drama establishes their deep bond; Naotaka protects the physically smaller Naiki from bullies, while Naiki teaches the illiterate Naotaka how to read poetry, forging a brotherhood that becomes the series' emotional core.

====Fourth Hour: "I Want To Become Stronger"====
The final hour of the prequel bridges the gap between childhood and the teenage years. Naiki returns to [[Yokosuka]] but struggles to fit in with the hyper-masculine Honda samurai, who mock his "soft" nature and reliance on his grandfather. Desperate to prove himself, a teenage Shigetomo (now played by [[Shinobu Otake]]) begs to join the army, but Shigetada refuses, dismissing him as "too soft" for the carnage of war.

In a pivotal scene, Shigetada visits his son in secret to explain his reasoning, imparting the central philosophy of the Honda clan: "The antler that strikes is broken; the antler that endures is polished." The prequel concludes with a massive cliffhanger as the Honda army mobilizes for the First Invasion of Yagikoshu in 1589. Shigetomo looks back at his mother one last time, narrating the transition into the main series:
{{Quote|text="I left a boy, seeking glory. I did not know I was marching into a river of blood that would never wash off."|sign=Shigetomo's narration}}

===Spring Season: The Loss of Innocence (Episodes 1–13)===
The "Spring Season" covers the late Sengoku period (1589–1599), depicting Shigetomo's transition from an innocent idealist to a cold pragmatist, driven by the trauma of losing his mentor.

The season opens with the '''Invasion of Yagikoshu''', where the young Shigetomo serves in the logistics corps, shielded from the front lines. The narrative tension builds toward '''Episode 10 ("Farewell, Anjō-sama")''', widely considered the emotional peak of the first cour. The episode depicts the '''Battle of Saikawa''' (1589), where Anjō Motonobu, leading the Left Army, is trapped in the river by Uesugi forces. The direction intercuts between Shigetomo waiting anxiously for news and Motonobu's final, non-violent stand. Swept away by the current and bleeding, Motonobu washes up in a peasant village. He dies not as a warrior, but as a man, thanking a merchant for a cup of tea. When Shigetomo receives the news—and is presented with Motonobu's tattered, armless armor—he destroys his own quarters in a fit of grief.

This trauma crystallizes in '''Episode 12 ("Punishment")'''. Shigetomo comes to realize that his father-in-law died because the Honda clan's logistics were too weak to support him. He adopts the persona of "The Punisher" ({{nihongo|Oshioki|お仕置き}}). In a shocking scene that silences the Honda war council, he personally executes a corrupt retainer who stole rice meant for the troops.
{{Quote|text="Kindness killed the giant. I will not be kind. From this day forth, the law is the only lord I serve."|sign=Shigetomo executing the traitor}}

The season concludes with '''Episode 13 ("Who Will Do It?")''', where Shigetada is crowned [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi]]. Recognizing his son's new ruthlessness, Shigetada offers Shigetomo the position of heir to the military shogunate, separating him from the Imperial line. Shigetomo accepts, effectively ending his childhood.

===Summer Season: The Golden Age (Episodes 14–26)===
The "Summer Season" shifts genres from war drama to political thriller, covering the chaotic unification wars (1600–1610) and the establishment of the Shogunate.

The arc begins with the "Friends at War" storyline in '''Episodes 14–15 ("The Battle of Ishikawada")''', where a territorial dispute forces Shigetomo to fight against his childhood friend Naotaka. Shigetomo defeats the Ii forces but spares Naotaka in '''Episode 17 ("Nakasendo")''', mirroring the mercy Naotaka showed him as a child. He convinces his father to absorb the Ii clan rather than destroy them, forging the "Honda-Ii Alliance."

The season highlights Shigetomo's intellect in '''Episode 20 ("The Governor from the Sea")'''. When the Insulonian governor [[Ernesto Lacuña]] arrives, Shigetomo adopts the "No-Sode" (sleeveless) armor style of his grandfather Motonobu to intimidate the foreigner physically, while simultaneously playing the fool to steal his maps.

The climax of the war arc arrives in '''Episode 24 ("Useless Victory")''', depicting the Battle of Tone. Shigetomo witnesses the "Crimson Fog" and the double betrayal that leads to the Honda victory. However, the sheer pile of bodies—including many friends—disgusts him. He vows to create a system where "numbers fight, not men," foreshadowing his future economic reforms. The season ends with '''Episode 26 ("Shogun of the Realm")''', where Shigetomo is invested as Shogun. He sits on the throne, looking powerful but utterly isolated, casting a shadow over the "Golden Age" to come.

===Fall Season: The Burning Palace (Episodes 27–40)===
The tone darkens significantly as the series enters the "Dual Sovereignty" era (1610–1625). The conflict shifts from the battlefield to the courtroom, culminating in the destruction of the Honda family unit.

'''Episodes 27–34''' depict Shigetomo as the "Bureaucrat Shogun." He implements the [[Sankei 325 Index|Rice Exchange]] and the '''Kinzō Reforms''', working himself to exhaustion. The drama portrays him alienating his wife [[Lady Saigō]] and his vassals, while the Ii clan elders begin to conspire against him, viewing his reforms as an insult to the samurai code. The friction reaches a breaking point in '''Episode 35 ("The Kinzō Reforms")''', where Shigetada, ruling from Toyozawa, accuses his son of trying to "buy the country with rice."

The tragedy is set in motion in '''Episode 38 ("The Unsent Letter")'''. Shigetomo writes a letter to Naotaka explaining that the reforms are meant to save the samurai class, not destroy it. A spy within the castle burns the letter before it reaches Naotaka, leading the Ii clan to believe they have been betrayed.

The season culminates in the 60-minute special '''Episode 40 ("Night of Blue Fire")''', depicting the '''Kinzō Incident''' of June 6, 1625. Ii Naotaka leads his troops to Toyozawa to "petition" the Retired Emperor, but agitators turn the march into an assault. [[Tetsuya Takeda]] gives a powerhouse final performance as the elderly Shigetada, holding the bridge of Toyozawa Castle alone with the ''[[Tonbogiri]]'' before being cut down by musket fire. Shigetomo arrives at the ruins at dawn. Seeing his father's bullet-riddled body, the trauma of Motonobu's death and the stress of ruling collide. His face begins to spasm—the first appearance of the "Otake Twitch"—and he screams his order to the sky:
{{Quote|text="Burn them all! The Red Devils, the castles, the memories—burn them all! Leave nothing but ash!"|sign=Shigetomo's descent into madness}}

===Winter season (Climax)===
The "Winter Season" (Episodes 41–50) is widely regarded by critics as the most distinct tonal shift in the history of the show. Departing from the political intrigue of the "Summer" arc and the action of the "Fall" arc, the final ten episodes transform into a psychological horror tragedy. The lighting became noticeably darker, utilizing high-contrast ''chiaroscuro'' techniques, and the dialogue became sparse.

This arc documents Shigetomo's rapid descent into madness ({{nihongo|kyōki|狂気}}) following the [[Kinzō Incident]]. The narrative focuses on his inability to reconcile his love for his friend [[Ii Naotaka]] with his duty to avenge his father, [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Shigetada]].

====Shigetomo's Descent====
Episodes 41 through 43 ("Silence in Okami" to "Sake and Ghosts") depict Shigetomo abandoning the bureaucracy he spent his life building.
* '''The "Otake Twitch":''' In Episode 42 ("The Punisher Returns"), lead actor [[Shinobu Otake]] introduced a physical tic that became a cultural phenomenon. Whenever the name "Naotaka" or "Ii" was mentioned, the muscles under Otake's right eye would spasm violently, followed by a chilling, unnatural smile. This improvisation, known as the "Otake Twitch" ({{nihongo|Otake-bikku|大竹ビック}}), effectively conveyed Shigetomo's fracturing psyche without dialogue.
* '''Hallucinations:''' In Episode 43, Shigetomo begins to see the ghost of his father, Shigetada (played by [[Tetsuya Takeda]] in white makeup), sitting in the war council. The ghost never speaks but simply stares at Shigetomo with disappointment. This leads to the famous scene where Shigetomo smashes a sake jar against the wall, screaming at empty air while his retainers watch in terrified silence.

{{Quote|box|quote="There is no Shogun here! There is only a son who let his father burn! Do not speak to me of laws, Muneshige. Laws are for the living. We are already ghosts."|source=— '''Honda Shigetomo''' to [[Hotta Muneshige]] (Episode 42)}}

====The March to Ichijoudani====
Episodes 44 through 46 cover the disastrous mobilization. The show portrays the Honda army not as a unified force, but as a confused rabble terrified of their own commander.
* '''The Rain Scene (Ep 45):''' In "The Red Devils," the Honda army is bogged down in torrential rain. Shigetomo, refusing to stop, beats his own horse to death in a frenzy. The camera lingers on [[Ken Takakura]] (Hotta Muneshige) watching his lord with a mixture of pity and resolve, setting up his mutiny in the next episode.
* '''The Split (Ep 46):''' "Hotta's Decision" focuses on the tactical mutiny where Hotta moves the army to [[Minatokoriyama]] to save them from Shigetomo's suicidal orders. The episode ends with a split-screen: Naotaka praying at a shrine in Ichijoudani, and Shigetomo laughing hysterically in his tent as he burns tactical maps.

====The Final Days====
Episodes 47 through 49 serve as the prelude to the finale.
* '''"Farewell, Father" (Ep 47):''' Shigetomo visits the burnt ruins of [[Toyozawa]]. In a departure from historical records, the show depicts Shigetomo finding a fragment of his father's antlered helmet. He breaks down weeping, the first time the character shows vulnerability since Episode 40.
* '''The Two Letters (Ep 48):''' In "Unprecedented Fate," the audience sees Shigetomo write the two contradictory letters—the threat and the forgiveness. The tension is heightened by the use of silence; the only sound in the scene is the scratching of the brush and Shigetomo's labored breathing.
* '''The Siege Begins (Ep 49):''' The episode "Ichijōdani" is almost entirely an action set piece, focusing on the Ii clan's desperate defense. It contrasts the disciplined, sorrowful defense of Ii Naotaka against the chaotic, rage-fueled attacks ordered by Shigetomo.

{{Quote|box|quote="I do not fight the Honda. I fight the demon that has possessed my friend. Aim for the banners, not the men! We kill only to wake him from his nightmare!"|source=— '''Ii Naotaka''' rallying his troops (Episode 49)}}
====Episode 50====
{{main|Episode 50 (Honda Shigetomo)}}
[[File:Honda Shigetomo Episode 50 climax.png|250px|thumb|left|Ii Naotaka (Kōji Ishizaka) deperately tending a dying Shigetomo.]]

The 90-minute finale, aired without commercial interruption on December 19, 1983, is considered a watershed moment in Sanese television history. Directed by Takafumi Miura with an almost total absence of background music, the episode depicts the tragic events of June 18, 1625, with unflinching realism.

The narrative opens with the Honda army paralyzed by torrential rain and low morale. Following the death of his loyal retainer Kondo Tadakatsu, Shigetomo (Shinobu Otake) recovers from his "Winter Season" psychosis. In a quiet, twenty-minute sequence, he drafts the famous "Letter of Forgiveness" to [[Ii Naotaka]] (Kōji Ishizaka).

In the climax, Shigetomo approaches [[Ichijoudani]] Castle alone. Contrary to the popular legend of him going unarmed, the drama depicts him wearing full, dark lacquered O-yoroi armor, visually emphasizing the crushing weight of his position. The reunion between the two friends is cut short when a nervous Ii ''ashigaru'' accidentally fires his arquebus, piercing Shigetomo's breastplate.

The scene where Naotaka frantically removes Shigetomo's armor, revealing the white silk underrobe (''shitagi'') completely sodden with blood, became an iconic image of the era. Upon reading the letter and realizing the war was a misunderstanding, Naotaka commits ''seppuku'' beside his dying friend. The episode ends with a silent montage of their joint funeral and the wind blowing through the hemp rope connecting their graves.

===Epilogue: ''The Ichijoudani Trinity''===
Following the unprecedented 90.4% viewership of Episode 50, [[KTK]] was inundated with calls from viewers demanding to know the fate of the Honda and Ii clans. In response, the network commissioned an emergency three-part epilogue special, titled '''''The Ichijoudani Trinity''''' ({{nihongo|一乗谷三部作|Ichijōdani Sanbusaku}}).

Aired consecutively on the night of December 24, 1983, this 3-hour television event deals with the immediate political fallout of the "Double Suicide" and the tense armistice negotiations between the grieving families.

