====Part I: The Morning of Silence====
The story resumes immediately after the credits of Episode 50. The bodies of [[Honda Shigetomo]] and [[Ii Naotaka]] are carried back to their respective camps. The shock of their deaths paralyzes the battlefield. 

[[Honda Shigetoki]] (Go Kato), Shigetomo's younger brother and successor, arrives from the rear guard. Overcome with grief and rage, he initially orders the total annihilation of the Ii clan, blaming them for "luring the Shogun to his death." However, the Ii forces, now led by Naotaka's young son [[Ii Naoyoshi]] (Koichi Sato), refuse to surrender, fortifying themselves in the ruins of the castle. 

Just as muskets are loaded for a renewed slaughter, [[Hotta Muneshige]] rides between the lines unarmed. He declares that the "Double Suicide" was a sacred act of reconciliation, and any man who fires a shot will be cursing the Shogun's soul. An uneasy 24-hour ceasefire is established.

====Part II: The Mediator====
The second hour is a tense courtroom drama set in a neutral temple halfway between the camps.
* The Honda Demand: Shigetoki demands the total surrender of the Ii lands. He proposes moving the Ii clan to the remote, impoverished [[Seto]] domain (reduced to a mere 10,000 ''koku''), effectively ending them as a political power.
* The Ii Rebuttal: Naoyoshi refuses, arguing that moving would starve his people. He insists on remaining in [[Ichijoudani]] to guard his father's grave, threatening a fight to the death if forced to leave.

The negotiations reach a deadlock. In the episode's climax, Hotta Muneshige loses his temper. He kicks open the sliding doors to reveal the coffins of Shigetomo and Naotaka in the courtyard. Pointing at the bodies, he delivers a blistering monologue:
{{Quote|text="Look at them! They died to end this cycle! If you fight again, you are not warriors; you are grave robbers stealing their peace! Do not let them die in vain!"|sign=Hotta Muneshige}}
He proposes a "Third Way": The Ii will surrender the northern strategic half of their territory to the Honda (stripping them of military capability) but will keep the southern fertile lands to feed their populace. Crucially, the Ii will become direct subordinates to the Honda, bypassing the feudal hierarchy to heal the rift.

====Part III: The Hemp Rope====
The final hour focuses on emotional reconciliation. Shigetoki and Naoyoshi agree to Muneshige's terms.

In a private moment, Naoyoshi reveals his father's final diary entries to Shigetoki. He confesses that Naotaka had been "completely unstable" in the days leading up to the battle, terrified that he would be forced to kill his best friend. Naoyoshi reveals that Naotaka had prepared to commit ''seppuku'' the moment the war started, solely to spare Shigetomo the pain of fighting him.

Shigetoki, realizing that his brother Shigetomo was loved even by his "enemies," breaks down. He remarks, "My brother would have given the whole country away just to see the people happy. We must build that happiness for him."

The special concludes with the joint funeral. Shigetoki and Naoyoshi tie a hemp rope between the two grave markers—the origin of the modern tradition. The narrator closes the series by stating that while the Honda Shogunate ruled for centuries, its foundation was not built on iron, but on the friendship of two men in Ichijoudani.

==Cast==
The drama features an all-star cast typical of [[KTK]] productions, blending veteran film actors with rising television stars.

===The Honda Clan===
* [[Shinobu Otake]] as '''[[Honda Shigetomo]]''' (Childhood name: '''Naiki''')
** The protagonist. The second Shogun of Okaiken. This role cemented Otake's status as the finest actor of his generation. Known previously for gritty crime dramas, his transformation into the fragile, aristocratic Shogun was initially doubted by critics but became legendary.
** [[Ryūnosuke Kamiki]] (child actor) as '''Young Naiki'''
* [[Tetsuya Takeda]] as '''[[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]]'''
** Shigetomo's father, later Emperor Shirakawa. A fierce warrior known as "The Antlered Demon."
* [[Masako Natsume]] as '''[[Hatsune-no-kata]]'''
** Shigetomo's mother and daughter of Anjō Motonobu. She serves as the emotional anchor of the Honda family.
* [[Go Kato]] as '''[[Honda Shigetoki]]'''
** Shigetomo's younger brother. A loyal and level-headed administrator who eventually succeeds his brother as the third Shogun.
* [[Hiroshi Mizugawa]] as '''Prince Takahito''' (Later '''Emperor Daigo''')
** Shigetomo's elder brother. The "Golden Child" who ascends the throne, creating the rift between the Imperial Court and the Shogunate.
* [[Yuko Tanaka]] as '''Lady Saigō'''
** Shigetomo's wife. A politically savvy woman from the Saigō clan who helps Shigetomo navigate the court bureaucracy.
* [[Kaoru Yachigusa]] as '''Lady Yugao'''
** Shigetada's concubine and mother of Shigetoki. A quiet but manipulative presence in the inner palace.

====Retainers of the Honda====
* [[Ken Takakura]] as '''[[Hotta Muneshige]]'''
** The Chief Retainer. A stoic warrior who attempts to mediate the conflict between Shigetomo and Naotaka. He acts as the "conscience" of the army.
* [[Bunta Sugawara]] as '''Kondo Tadakatsu'''
** Shigetomo's personal bodyguard and childhood caretaker. He dies protecting Shigetomo in the final episode, breaking the Shogun's psychosis.
* [[Sano Yuma]] as '''Sakata Ishiemon'''
** Shigetomo's second-in-command and scribe. He is the only person present when Shigetomo writes his final letters.
