==Production==
The production of ''Honda Shigetomo'' is widely regarded as the most ambitious undertaking in the history of [[KTK]] and Sanese television. With a budget of ¥7.5 billion, it remains the most expensive Taiga drama ever produced per episode.

===Casting Shinobu Otake===
The casting of [[Shinobu Otake]] as Shigetomo was a major gamble for KTK. At the time, Otake was known primarily for playing violent yakuza enforcers and unstable rebels in independent cinema. Traditionalists felt he lacked the grace and nobility required for a Taiga lead. 

Director Takafumi Miura fought for Otake, arguing that Shigetomo was not a traditional hero, but a "dangerous radical" who dismantled the samurai class. Miura believed only Otake possessed the "manic intelligence" to make the character's descent into madness believable.

To prepare for the role, Otake underwent a grueling physical transformation. He lost 15 kilograms to portray the gaunt, stressed Shogun of the "Winter Season." He also isolated himself from the rest of the cast for the final three months of filming, refusing to speak to Kōji Ishizaka (Naotaka) off-camera to cultivate a genuine sense of alienation.

===The "Burning of Toyozawa"===
For the climax of the "Fall Season" (Episode 40), the production team constructed a 1:1 scale replica of the Toyozawa Castle keep on a vacant lot in [[Fukui Prefecture]]. Rather than using miniatures or optical effects, production designer Yoshio Harada insisted on burning the actual structure. 

The sequence was filmed in a single take using twelve cameras. Pyrotechnicians used a mixture of gasoline and copper sulfate to create the episode's signature "blue fire." The heat was so intense that the eyebrows of actor [[Tetsuya Takeda]] (playing Shigetada) were singed off during his final monologue. The footage of the collapsing burning tower is considered one of the most iconic images in Sanese cinema and is still used in KTK station identification bumpers today.

===The "Tomita Sound"===
Composer [[Isao Tomita]] revolutionized the soundscape of period dramas with his score. Breaking away from the traditional orchestral swelling used in previous Taiga dramas, Tomita utilized the [[Moog synthesizer]] III-P and the Roland Jupiter-8 to create an ambient, electronic atmosphere. 
* '''The "River of Tears":''' The main theme combined a digitized Shakuhachi flute with a synthesized heartbeat that accelerated as the song progressed, symbolizing Shigetomo's anxiety.
* '''The Sound of Madness:''' For the "Winter Season," Tomita sampled the sounds of screaming crows and grinding metal, playing them backward at slow speeds to create the background drone heard whenever Shigetomo suffered a hallucination.

==Critical Reception==
''Honda Shigetomo'' was not merely a television show; it was a social phenomenon.

===The "Shigetomo Lock-in"===
Viewership ratings began strong at 50.5% and climbed steadily throughout the year. By the "Winter Season," the show had captivated the entire nation. The finale, aired on December 19, 1983, achieved a 90.4% household share. This event is known as the "Shigetomo Lock-in" ({{nihongo|Shigetomo-gikomori|重朝籠もり}}). Police reported that not a single violent crime was committed in the capital city of [[Okami]] during the three-hour broadcast.

===The "Otake Twitch"===
Shinobu Otake's performance is cited as one of the greatest in Adonian acting history. His improvisation in Episode 42—where the muscles under his right eye spasmed violently upon hearing Ii Naotaka's name—became iconic. This facial tic, known as the "Otake Twitch" ({{nihongo|Otake-bikku|大竹ビック}}), became a cultural shorthand for suppressed rage.

Film critic Osamu Takatoshi wrote: "Otake does not play Shigetomo; he is possessed by him. It is a terrifying, masculine performance that strips away the armor of the samurai to reveal the frightened boy underneath."

===Critical Analysis===
Contemporary critics praised the series for deconstructing the "Samurai Myth." Unlike previous dramas that glorified feudal loyalty, ''Honda Shigetomo'' portrayed the shogunate as a crushing bureaucracy built on trauma. 
* Cultural critic Osamu Takatoshi wrote in ''The Okami Times'': "Shinobu Otake has not just played a Shogun; she has skinned the history books and shown us the shivering human nerves underneath. It is a masterpiece of psychological horror masquerading as history."
* The show was also noted for its visual language. The transition from the bright, golden lighting of the "Summer Season" to the monochromatic, high-contrast mud of the "Winter Season" is cited in film schools as a masterclass in visual storytelling.

==Legacy==
The impact of ''Honda Shigetomo'' extended far beyond the entertainment industry, fundamentally altering the political landscape of [[Okaiken]] during the 1980s.

===Political Repercussions===
The drama's sympathetic portrayal of the Honda clan as tragic reformers rather than feudal tyrants triggered a phenomenon known as the "Honda Boom" ({{nihongo|Honda-būmu|本多ブーム}}). This surge in popularity coincided with the political rise of [[Honda Shigehara]], the direct descendant of Shigetomo and leader of the opposition [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO).

Political analysts noted that Shigehara's public approval ratings correlated directly with the broadcast of the "Summer Season" (depicting the Golden Age) and the "Winter Season" (depicting the tragedy). Shigehara, who had previously been viewed by the public as a stiff aristocrat, was suddenly seen as the heir to a legacy of "self-sacrifice for the nation."

====The "Taiga Diet" Incident====
The show's influence was so pervasive that it halted proceedings in the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]]. On February 14, 1983, during a heated Budget Committee session, ruling party Prime Minister [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]] (LDP) attempted to attack the PPO's economic platform.

In response, Honda Shigehara quoted his ancestor's line from Episode 17 ("The Tea House Debate"): "Ink is sharper than steel, Prime Minister." The quote received thunderous applause from the gallery, forcing a temporary recess. Later that year, conservative MPs attempted to censure [[KTK]], accusing the network of broadcasting "pro-Honda propaganda." This backfired spectacularly, leading to a dip in the ruling party's polls and a surge in PPO memberships.

===Electoral Impact===
The "Shigetomo Effect" is widely credited with revitalizing the PPO's fortunes throughout the 1980s. Following the drama's airing in 1983, the party saw a consistent upward trend in seat counts, culminating in the historic 1990 election victory where Honda Shigehara became Prime Minister—the second Honda to rule Okaiken since the 19th century, after his brother Honda Tadashige.

